+visibility-choked (the compiler). Between it and reference-choked stands
+checker-choked, where a linter the project runs refuses the bypass; its
+section is below. Python's ladder is in its own section below too.
+
+## TypeScript's linter rung
+
+A module is visible to every file, so the compiler never refuses an import
+of it. The project's linter can: `@posthog/coherence/lint` is an ESLint v9
+API plugin named `coherence` with one rule, `coherence/chokepoint`, which
+oxlint loads through `jsPlugins`:
+
+```jsonc
+{
+  "jsPlugins": ["@posthog/coherence/lint"],
+  "rules": { "coherence/chokepoint": ["error", { "root": "." }] }
+}
+```
+
+The rule reads the spec, so the chokepoint is declared once: every
+`*.spec.md` under `root` (relative to the lint's working folder, the folder
+itself by default), outside the config's `ignore`, through Coherence's own
+spec parser. It acts only on a bullet whose `protects:` is a module path and
+whose `chokepoint:` is a module path or `Name in file.ts`; a bare symbol
+needs the language server and stays with Coherence's check. It follows the
+check's import rulings (d-7abd1ba8): an `import`, `import x = require()`,
+`typeof import()` or `import()` of the protected module from any file but
+the chokepoint's module is a bypass, and an `export … from` or `export *`
+of it is a re-export wherever it stands. A file in a test folder, or named
+`.test` or `.spec`, is exempt. Specifiers resolve relative to the file, or
+through `compilerOptions.paths` in the nearest `tsconfig.json` above the
+file, within the root (patterns with at most one `*`), since a monorepo
+maps one alias differently per package; the rule never uses type
+information.
+
+Nothing is dropped in silence. A spec it cannot read, a module-form bullet
+whose protected module or chokepoint it cannot resolve, and a root that
+holds no readable `coherence.config.json` are reported at line 1 of each
+linted file under the component, never thrown, since a plugin that throws
+fails the whole lint. Linted from a package folder, the rule needs `root`
+set to the project root (`"root": ".."` from `nodejs/`), and says so when
+it is not.
+
+The TypeScript adapter grades a clean module chokepoint **checker-choked**,
+with `oxlint (<config>) rule coherence/chokepoint` as the enforcer, when the
+rule's own reading of the specs guards that protected module with that
+chokepoint's module, and an `.oxlintrc.json` or `.oxlintrc.*.json` under the
+root loads the plugin (by its package specifier or a path to the module),
+sets the rule to error in its top-level `rules`, and holds the protected
+module in its folder. The config's folder is taken as the lint's working
+folder, so the rule's `root` must resolve from there to the project root. An
+override does not count, and neither does a config under which a nested
+`.oxlintrc.json` leaves the rule out: each lints part of the workspace, and
+a chokepoint covers all of it. In every other case the grade is what it
+was. The refutation is Coherence's own, as for Python's checker rung; the
+rule's tests witness the linter going red on a staged import and green
+without it.
 
